<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Pergamon</h1><p class="st-meta">282 BCE – 133 BCE · capital Pergamon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hellenistic kingdom of the Attalid dynasty in western Anatolia, a great centre of art and learning that was bequeathed to Rome in 133 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philetaerus">Philetaerus</a>, an officer guarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>' treasure of 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> at the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, changed sides to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> in 282 BCE and kept the fortress and money. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-i">Eumenes I</a> asserted independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> took the title of king around 238 BCE after defeating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galatia">Galatians</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> into a showpiece capital of terraces, temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, with a library second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria's</a>, traditionally linked to the development of parchment. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon-altar">Great Altar of Zeus</a> celebrated victory over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galatia">Galatians</a>. Loyal alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> wide lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Peace of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> died in 133 BCE leaving his kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus">Aristonicus</a>, claiming to be a royal son, led a revolt that was crushed by 129 BCE, and the kingdom became the Roman province of Asia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon-altar">Pergamon Altar</a> is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-pergamon.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Pergamon" width="320" height="185"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Pergamon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>282 BCE – 133 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pergamon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Attalus I, Eumenes II, Attalus III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Koine Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Attalid Kingdom, Pergamene Kingdom, Pergamum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-208&amp;focus=kingdom-of-pergamon" aria-label="Kingdom of Pergamon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5353 775 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Pergamon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5673 1031L5809 1004M5673 1031L5682 1070M5673 1031L5675 1054M5673 1031L5668 1043M5673 1031L5659 984M5673 1031L6140 1232M5673 1031L5304 942M5673 1031L5622 1073"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5809 1004h0M5682 1070h0M5675 1054h0M5668 1043h0M5659 984h0M6140 1232h0M5304 942h0M5622 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5673 1031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5673 1031h0"/></svg><